WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:03.630
(upbeat electronic music)

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.668
- Welcome to Ear Biscuits, I'm Rhett.

00:00:05.668 --> 00:00:07.580
- And I'm Link.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.270
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:14.324
we're asking the question
how did we survive

00:00:14.324 --> 00:00:16.420
before cell phones?

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.607
- Or mobile phones.
- Or smart phones.

00:00:19.607 --> 00:00:21.571
Mobile phones.

00:00:21.571 --> 00:00:23.800
- Or, no, that's all I can think of.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.830
- I think that's what we call phones.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.469
- Yeah this is a little something

00:00:27.469 --> 00:00:30.445
that we're gonna try today,
a little something different

00:00:30.445 --> 00:00:33.599
where we're actually going to,

00:00:33.599 --> 00:00:37.210
we're calling this a
nostalgia, a nostalgia tour.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.450
How do you say that word?
- Nostalgia.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.578
- You don't say nostalgia.
- Nostalgia, no.

00:00:41.578 --> 00:00:43.950
- Yeah 'cause it's like
when you get nauseous

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.951
thinking about things from
the past, you get nostalgious.

00:00:47.951 --> 00:00:49.560
(chuckles)

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.890
- I didn't know that I felt
that sometimes, but I do,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.590
I mean we may feel that over
the course of reminiscing

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:00.610
over our relationship with
phones before cell phones.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.860
- That's an interesting
term, relationships.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.040
- Because I just might
feel sick to my stomach

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.570
that this is how we used to live

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:10.690
or that I wish I still lived that way.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.323
I haven't quite figured that
out but what I do know is,

00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:18.185
frequently on Ear Biscuits,
we'll reminisce about things

00:01:18.185 --> 00:01:21.506
so I just thought let's
reminisce within this category

00:01:21.506 --> 00:01:24.608
of what phones used to be like.

00:01:24.608 --> 00:01:27.920
- Right and so we've
thought of some things like,

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.987
remember when--
- Well don't say 'em yet.

00:01:29.987 --> 00:01:32.430
- I'm not, I'm gonna make blanks.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.430
I'm just making blanks.
- 'Cause I have my list,

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.180
you have your list.
- Remember when

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.330
blankety-blank-blank
blanka-blank-blank phones?

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:40.930
So that's how it's gonna
be and then it'll be like,

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:43.810
remember when
blankety-blank-blank blank phones.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.140
- Right.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:49.550
- But before we reminisce, let's
talk about the present-day.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:53.280
I mean let's talk about the
reality of being tethered.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.790
- Yeah I mean--
- Ironically,

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.750
you're tethered wirelessly,
wirelessly tethered.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.863
Think about that, put that on a t-shirt.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:02.913
I'm tethered to a wireless phone.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.400
- That's not really a sellable
t-shirt I don't think.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.340
Yeah last night Christy
and I went out on a date

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:14.550
and we're driving to
the restaurant, I mean,

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.387
when we're at the table having a date,

00:02:17.387 --> 00:02:20.490
we're not that couple
who's just on our phones

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:21.960
and not talking to each other.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.740
But there are some times
when something will come up

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.570
in conversation and the phone
will become a third party

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:28.661
to our date, like a third
wheel where it's like,

00:02:28.661 --> 00:02:31.424
oh there's unlimited
information on this thing.

00:02:31.424 --> 00:02:34.392
But--
- The challenge is

00:02:34.392 --> 00:02:36.963
if you have to go to
the phone in the midst

00:02:36.963 --> 00:02:38.840
of a conversation which happens often

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.830
and there are legitimate reasons to defer

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.740
to the internet in the
midst of a conversation,

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:45.550
often during an argument to get to a fact.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:47.698
Argue with my wife all
the time about facts.

00:02:47.698 --> 00:02:48.850
(chuckles) No I don't, you know,

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.180
just any time you need
to reference something.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:54.610
The temptation once you have
access to all the information

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.690
in the world, you get your
information and you're like,

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:57.630
well what's on Twitter?

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.678
Did somebody like my photo?
- We were driving.

00:03:00.678 --> 00:03:02.030
- Can't do that.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:05.990
- We were driving and we were
already having a serious,

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.190
not a, we weren't having an argument.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:09.850
- Just a serious--
- We were having like a--

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.010
- Discussion.
- Let's share things that are

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.470
in our hearts and minds with each other.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.070
Let's get into that mode even

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:17.320
before we're at the restaurant.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.030
- Did it happen naturally
or did you prompt it

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.210
like a youth pastor?
- I'd like to share something

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.140
from my heart and mind.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.082
- Or was it just natural?
- It was just natural--

00:03:25.082 --> 00:03:26.917
- Good.
- Our relationship is

00:03:26.917 --> 00:03:31.120
as vibrant as ever and
that's not facetious

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.457
even though it sounded that way.

00:03:32.457 --> 00:03:34.180
(Rhett chuckles)

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:35.990
Yeah we were just talking.
- You were just talking

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:37.630
like two normal people on a date.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:39.711
- And I look over and she's on her phone.

00:03:39.711 --> 00:03:41.240
- Oh--
- Unlike me,

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.147
she can do more than one thing at once.

00:03:43.147 --> 00:03:44.890
So I do respect that.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:46.780
- She's also, she's not driving so.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:47.820
- And she's not driving.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.120
I was driving and talking,
that's two things.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.687
- Well you don't do that well.

00:03:51.687 --> 00:03:56.629
- I was kind of making
an introspective point

00:03:56.629 --> 00:04:00.420
and I thought it was a
very great opportunity

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.080
for connection and she was listening

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.870
but when I looked over there
and she was on her phone,

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:06.320
I was like, do you have to
be on your phone right now,

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.460
I'm kinda trying to make
a point about something.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.200
This feels important to
me and she's like well--

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.220
- Look at this dog hug this cat.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:18.220
- No, she was actually
talking to the babysitter,

00:04:18.832 --> 00:04:21.380
coordinating the time that
we would come back home

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:25.810
so it was a legitimate
exercise but I was like,

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.300
'cause she was like, she has to leave

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.553
and now I know when we're getting back

00:04:29.553 --> 00:04:33.302
and I have to tell her and
she needs to know right now.

00:04:33.302 --> 00:04:36.903
So I was barking up the wrong
tree but I did feel like,

00:04:38.261 --> 00:04:40.476
you know, before the cell phone,

00:04:40.476 --> 00:04:42.551
you would coordinate all that,

00:04:42.551 --> 00:04:45.450
you'd have to go through all
these great lengths to do it

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.103
but you weren't constantly
in conversation.

00:04:47.103 --> 00:04:49.091
- Well and once you were
gone, you were gone.

00:04:49.091 --> 00:04:51.080
You were gone.
- Yeah.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:52.620
Unless it was an emergency.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.440
Even if it was.
- What'd you do?

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:56.230
Tell people what
restaurant you're going to.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:57.550
I guess some parents did.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.650
We were never parents without cell phones,

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.180
so we don't know what it
was like to be a parent.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.490
First of all, I don't remember my parents

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:06.850
having a date night.

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:10.100
That's like a modern invention. (chuckles)

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.220
But would they have told a babysitter,

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.180
they probably said, this
is what we're going to do

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.600
and this is when we're going to be back.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.355
But they didn't say--
- I'm paying you to be

00:05:18.355 --> 00:05:20.440
completely in charge.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.170
- Yeah, right, handle everything.

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.780
- I'm not paying you to then text me

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.953
with every little thing.
- Sidebar.

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.586
I mean, your daughter is 15 years old.

00:05:30.586 --> 00:05:32.072
- Yeah.

00:05:32.072 --> 00:05:34.090
- Why doesn't she just do the babysitting?

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:36.746
She doesn't wanna take care of the kids?

00:05:36.746 --> 00:05:38.393
- Well that's a good question.

00:05:39.673 --> 00:05:42.190
That just doesn't work a lot of times.

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:43.110
I'll just put it that way.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:45.438
- 'Cause I'm thinking about
instituting my date night

00:05:45.438 --> 00:05:48.108
and just not having any childcare.

00:05:48.108 --> 00:05:50.050
- Oh, we do that.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.580
The kids are old enough
to take care of themselves

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.820
or Lily and Lincoln to
help watch over Lando,

00:05:55.820 --> 00:06:00.230
but none of them can drive
and there's like logistics

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.470
that are happening like they
had to go to different places

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:03.970
and stuff like that.
- Right.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:06.960
- So we have--
- Sometimes you gotta

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:07.800
cover it with an adult.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.590
- We have an ongoing arrangement
so then she helps out

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.160
with things like date night too

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.760
but it's a much larger scope than that.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.570
But I look over at Christy,
I'm like okay, okay,

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:18.941
I'm not gonna jump down
your throat about this.

00:06:18.941 --> 00:06:21.990
But then I swear when I
looked over there again,

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.702
she was on Instagram.

00:06:23.702 --> 00:06:25.180
I think so.

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:26.883
- Oh my wife is constantly on.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:31.079
Well what my wife found
this year was Twitter

00:06:31.079 --> 00:06:36.079
and that is just, I mean,
she's a Twitter hawk, man.

00:06:36.666 --> 00:06:39.392
- But when there's, and
it's not like when we have

00:06:39.392 --> 00:06:42.130
a spare moment, you just
find yourself reaching

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:43.360
for your phone and (mimics whip lashing)

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:44.650
pulling that thing out.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:49.600
I mean it's almost a mindless
reflex to pull that thing out.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:50.673
- Oh yeah.

00:06:50.673 --> 00:06:51.950
In fact--
- Beyond it.

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:54.839
- I saw somebody tweet the other day,

00:06:54.839 --> 00:06:58.063
do you remember what it
was like to just poop?

00:06:58.986 --> 00:07:01.290
- Yeah without being on your phone.

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:03.980
- You know, what was it like to just poop?

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:08.320
Now I've made repeated pledges to myself

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:10.793
to not surf while pooping.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:13.940
But I just can't stick to it, man.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:16.260
- Have you tried putting,

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:18.020
have you tried pudding?

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:19.120
Have you tried putting--
- Pudding.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.824
It goes right through ya.

00:07:20.824 --> 00:07:23.670
- (chuckles) Have you tried putting a book

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:25.903
on the back of the toilet?

00:07:25.903 --> 00:07:29.483
And then you're tempted to read it.

00:07:29.483 --> 00:07:30.853
Have you tried that?

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:34.830
- Yeah well I used to read books.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:36.760
- But I mean now that...

00:07:38.593 --> 00:07:41.560
- I used to read books on
the toilet, let me clarify.

00:07:43.918 --> 00:07:45.610
- Have you tried pulling the phone,

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:48.180
putting the phone away
and pulling out a book?

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.585
- Not since the phone was a thing.

00:07:50.585 --> 00:07:53.210
I've taken a book to the crapper.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:55.240
- Yeah but then you're on it too long.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:56.480
Like the phone is the perfect thing,

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:57.500
you just get a little taste of--

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:58.950
- You shouldn't do any of that though

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:03.950
because sometimes you get
into an Instagram hole--

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:05.130
- And you're sitting there.
- Next thing you know,

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:06.740
you got hemorrhoids.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.150
You just can't do that, man.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:09.340
You shouldn't even take it in there.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:12.885
It shouldn't work, it should
be like those grocery stores

00:08:12.885 --> 00:08:17.160
that have some kind of
scrambler on the phones.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.430
I'm just convinced.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:20.423
What's happening in grocery stores?

00:08:20.423 --> 00:08:22.310
There's a scrambler on the phones.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:25.017
- They put one in the DMV, I
can pretty much believe that.

00:08:25.017 --> 00:08:27.470
- I'm pulling up recipes because I'm

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:30.970
at the freakin' grocery
store and I got no service,

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:32.963
I got like one X.

00:08:34.910 --> 00:08:35.852
What's happening there?

00:08:35.852 --> 00:08:38.434
- I don't think they
have any reason to block

00:08:38.434 --> 00:08:41.010
like cell signal in there.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:45.810
I think it's the exposed
steel of the ceiling.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:48.030
- You think it's being
blocked by just the structure.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.473
- But that wouldn't make sense.

00:08:49.473 --> 00:08:51.900
- Well how come you get
cell phone signals while

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.760
in buildings and offices--
- All types of structure.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.800
- Like on the first floor
of a large office building,

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:58.900
your cell phone works.

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.490
Why doesn't it work in a grocery store?

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:01.323
- Must be the groceries.
- And just the grocery--

00:09:01.323 --> 00:09:04.707
- I think it's the food.
- You think food is absorbing

00:09:04.707 --> 00:09:06.929
the cell signals?

00:09:06.929 --> 00:09:10.240
Yeah, maybe chips 'cause
there's a lot of chips.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:12.653
The chips aisle has
gotten nothing but chips.

00:09:12.653 --> 00:09:15.831
- Have you correlated the signal
to going in certain aisles?

00:09:15.831 --> 00:09:18.481
- I've definitely never
gotten it in the chips aisle.

00:09:19.429 --> 00:09:21.820
- Hmm, I don't know.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.610
I can pull out my phone
right now and Google it

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:26.400
but I'm not going to.
- I think we should just put

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:29.020
our phones in a bag of
chips and see if it works.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:33.180
- I mean this podcast is an exercise in...

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:37.260
I was gonna say stamina
but restraint is the word

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:38.093
I'm looking for.

00:09:38.093 --> 00:09:39.700
Sometimes it's an exercise in stamina.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:40.613
- Stamina.

00:09:40.613 --> 00:09:43.890
- (chuckles) I think some
listeners are nodding their head

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:46.410
right now like yeah, I can
barely get through this.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:50.700
But, restraint, because we're
not going to our phones,

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.307
we're just having a
conversation for like an hour

00:09:53.307 --> 00:09:55.610
with each other, every week.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:58.090
Boy that's healthy.
- I put my phone

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:01.150
on airplane mode when I
do this or airport mode

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:02.585
as you would say.
- Yeah I do.

00:10:02.585 --> 00:10:04.693
- When I'm doing a podcast.

00:10:04.693 --> 00:10:06.360
That's what I do.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.000
Well let's reminisce.
- Yeah 'cause I've got a list

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.360
of things just not, it's
not in direct contrast

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.683
to the mobile phones,
it's things that are like,

00:10:16.553 --> 00:10:17.386
until I started thinking about it,

00:10:17.386 --> 00:10:19.560
things I just almost forgot existed.

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:24.560
Like the way my life and
the world used to operate

00:10:24.729 --> 00:10:27.460
was so different.
- Yes and you'll find out

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:30.510
all about that in a second, but first,

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.910
we wanna let you know where
you can get this snazzy hoodie

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:35.053
that Link is wearing.

00:10:35.053 --> 00:10:36.440
- Look at that.
- Which sometimes,

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:38.257
for some reason I call hoodies beanies.

00:10:38.257 --> 00:10:40.870
They got a weird thing
in my brain that wants me

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:42.050
to call a hoodie beanies.

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:43.120
- Never been a beanie.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.671
- Yeah 'cause I love beanies so much.

00:10:45.671 --> 00:10:47.970
- There's nothing like
just wearing a shirt

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:50.660
and then realizing I could cover my head

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:51.650
with part of this shirt.

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:55.560
- And this hoodie has the
latest in hoodie technology.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.341
It's called the drawstring.

00:10:57.341 --> 00:10:58.550
Look at that.

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:00.550
It's a string that goes through the hood.

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:03.640
It can be tied, it can be left hanging.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.810
It can hang at different lengths,

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:08.960
at which point it will
distress some people

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:10.240
depending on their
personality and disposition.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:13.770
- Now my son told me that if
you tie your hoodies strings,

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:15.560
that means you're in a relationship.

