00:44:36.700 --> 00:44:39.700
- Okay so the Rolodex
which just to explain,

00:44:39.700 --> 00:44:44.440
it was basically this
ringed collection of cards

00:44:44.440 --> 00:44:47.061
that you could write numbers and names on

00:44:47.061 --> 00:44:51.270
and organize them by alphabetical
order and put anything,

00:44:51.270 --> 00:44:52.450
any information you want.

00:44:52.450 --> 00:44:55.050
This thing was invented by--

00:44:55.050 --> 00:44:56.540
- Roll E. Dex.

00:44:56.540 --> 00:44:58.963
- Arnold Neustadter.

00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:03.080
He was a 20th century
inventor from Brooklyn.

00:45:03.080 --> 00:45:08.080
Now he also invented,
before this, the Swivodex,

00:45:09.170 --> 00:45:11.960
which was a device that kept
ink bottles from spilling.

00:45:11.960 --> 00:45:13.720
I don't know for what purpose.

00:45:13.720 --> 00:45:16.823
- Well 'cause you don't
wanna spill your ink, man.

00:45:16.823 --> 00:45:19.590
- He also invented the Clipodex.

00:45:19.590 --> 00:45:21.490
- Oh wow.
- Sensing a theme here.

00:45:21.490 --> 00:45:23.080
Arnold.
- What is that for?

00:45:23.080 --> 00:45:24.120
Paper clips?
- It was a device that

00:45:24.120 --> 00:45:27.310
attached to the knees of stenographers.

00:45:27.310 --> 00:45:29.290
To keep their pads from moving.

00:45:29.290 --> 00:45:30.123
- Oh wow.

00:45:30.123 --> 00:45:32.670
- Pads, the ones that were on their knees.

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:35.740
And he also made, he changed things up.

00:45:35.740 --> 00:45:37.360
The Punched.

00:45:37.360 --> 00:45:38.650
The Punched.

00:45:38.650 --> 00:45:40.530
- Punched.
- Which was a hole-puncher,

00:45:40.530 --> 00:45:42.289
made holes in papers.

00:45:42.289 --> 00:45:45.150
Now, then he came along--
- Prolific inventor.

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:46.060
- With the Rolodex.

00:45:46.060 --> 00:45:48.721
Now the funny thing is is
there was an article in Gizmodo

00:45:48.721 --> 00:45:52.120
